# Observability

Golden reference: `src/middleware/requestContext.ts`, `src/middleware/requestLogger.ts`, `src/lib/observability/`.

## Purpose

Every request and side effect should be traceable without leaking secrets.

## Architecture decisions

- `requestContext` assigns `x-request-id` (from header or UUID) and stores it in `res.locals` + async context.
- `requestLogger` emits one structured `request_finished` log per HTTP response.
- Discord alert payloads pass through `redactAlertPayload` before leaving the process.

## Required patterns

| Concern | Location |
| ------- | -------- |
| `requestId` | `res.locals.requestId` / `runWithRequestContext` |
| HTTP access log | `logger.info("request_finished", { requestId, method, path, statusCode, durationMs })` |
| Service/job logs | Include `requestId` when available; add `operation`, `provider`, `durationMs` |
| Alert redaction | `redactAlertPayload` for webhook bodies; skip sensitive routes via `isSensitiveAlertBodyPath` |

## Good examples

```ts
logger.info("example_job_processed", {
  requestId: getRequestContext()?.requestId,
  jobId: job.id,
  exampleId: job.data.exampleId,
});

logger.error("r2_presign_failed", {
  requestId: getRequestContext()?.requestId,
  provider: "r2",
  operation: "presign",
  error,
});
```

## Bad examples

```ts
console.log("error", error);
logger.info("user login", { password: body.password });
logger.info("webhook", { headers: req.headers }); // may contain Authorization
```

## Explicit prohibitions

- No `console.log` in application code — use `src/app/logger.ts`.
- Never log passwords, tokens, API keys, cookies, or full auth headers.
- Do not attach raw request bodies to Discord alerts on `/auth` or `/uploads/presign` paths.

Full secret/env prohibitions: `security-secrets.mdc`.

## AI guidance

When adding external calls or jobs, log outcome + duration with stable event names (`snake_case`). Use `redactAlertPayload` before alerting with user-controlled data.
